<section class="ed-section">
  <div class="ed-container">
    <div class="ed-accent-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <h2 class="ed-section-label">Worktree pain points, solved</h2>
    <p class="ed-section-desc">
      Git worktrees are powerful, but managing them manually is painful. workmux
      automates the rough edges.
    </p>
    <div class="list">
      <article>
        <h3>"You need to reinstall everything"</h3>
        <p>
          New worktrees have no <code>.env</code>, <code>node_modules</code>, or
          dev server. workmux can <a
            href="/guide/configuration/#file-operations"
            >copy config files and symlink dependencies</a
          >, then <a href="/guide/configuration/#lifecycle-hooks"
            >run setup commands</a
          >.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>"You need to clean up after"</h3>
        <p>
          <code>workmux merge</code> merges the branch, deletes the worktree, closes
          the tmux window, and removes the local branch. The
          <a href="/guide/skills/#merge"><code>/merge</code> skill</a> can run the
          full workflow autonomously.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>"Conflicts arise on merge"</h3>
        <p>
          Conflicts remain a fact of git, but your agent can rebase, understand
          both sides, and resolve them. See
          <a href="/guide/workflows/#finishing-work">finishing work</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

